*** What use was a picture? He knew that someone would have a use for one - at least that's what he hoped as he was selling - but what was the actual use of one? It wasn't Netflix. It couldn't get you high or laid. But none of that mattred because *he* had a use for it. He could sell it. Maybe not for much, but enough. Enough for one packet, maybe two. Yeah. Two. Then he could take one back to Mary. Had to not use both himself. He promised himself. He started to wrap the picture back up in the paper, then he noticed that it was still wet. He rubbed his finger on the paint. It smelled like Castrol GTX. Is that what paint was supposed to smell like? Was it supposed to be wet? Are all paintings like that? He rubbed the paint a little more to smudge the mark his thumb has made. As he did he saw an eye. A beautiful eye. Like his mother looking at him those few times he'd been good. He couldn't help himself. He rubbed more. A nose. A foreheard - a high forehead. Blond curls and what looked like a scarf. It was beautiful. Too late to stop now. He got a tissue out of his pocket and rubbed more and more. It was the head of a woman in a blue dress, the kind they wore in the olden days. He didn't know art. He didn't know much. But he knew that what under the paint, was far more valuable that the grossly colured dawb on the surface.
** 2. Police procedural?
*** A cold wet morning of early March. The fog hung low and dank, like a shroud over the field. Climbing slowly over the stile, the man placed his foot carefully on the step. One hip operation was enough for one year, thank you. On the wet sludgey ground there were some remnants of rubbish that had been left. Fly tipping was getting worse every year. People seemingly making trips out into trhe countryside to offload the rubbish that they would be charged for at the tip. Old settees were not unusual. More frequently it was bits of detritus. The last unsellable remnants of a cleared out home. This was scraps of paper from old offices. He squelched his way through the sludge and the waving bits of paper. He checked the punnet of mushrooms. He'd dropped none of them at the style. He'd checked under the trees. That was where the cows sheltered. At the edge of the cover provided by the trees it was still damp enough fo the mushrooms. Their subterranean networks tested the ground for the right nutrients. You didn't get mushrooms in heavily farmed ground. It was always best to check fallow land. The scrappy fields left alone for livestock to graze. Untouched by chemicals. At magical moments, when the humidity was right, when the soild had just the right amount of acids and alkali those underground fungal networks would send out the signal calling for mushrooms to be formed. And up they'd spring. An ancient miracle. Circular fairy rings of mushroom tops. Some deadly too, it should be said. But not when you'd been looking for them as long as him. That was really why he came now. Not because he needed them for food, but because *he* could still find them. He could still enjoy the thrill of the hunt. As he crossed the field to the far side, down low where the water would run, settle and make a damp bed, he saw a white flash in the dirt. More rubbish no doubt. What had the fly tippers been leaving? It had spread across the field. It looked like a shop clearance. Old folders, the kinds that kept customer records decades before. Old bits of clothing. Pieces that had not sold and had then been left in a store room no doubt. The white flah he saw was a hand from a tailor's dummy. Reaching up from the soil, almost like it was gesturing for attention. They should be ashamed of themselves, leaving a mess like this. If they get paid to take someone's mess away, they should include something to cover the tipping fees. It was just greed and selfishness. He bent awkwarly and started to search for mushrooms among the detritus. He was looking for good white button mushrooms. You often found them in the cow manure. His wife wasn't keen on the idea, but he explained to her that all her food grew in cow manure. It's just mushrooms grew in it when it was a little fresher. Nothing here so he went over to where the dummies hand had been left. Odd that. A little pinkness on the hand. Not what you'd expect. Quite lifelike. Then he saw the ring on the ring finger.
** 3. Cosy?
*** Of course it would happen on the first day, wouldn't it? When the unpacking had barely started, and the cottage was in such a mess. It had not been a particularly eventful morning because, well, it had barely been a morning at all. She had only woken a few minutes previously. Just enough time to splash a few drops of water on her face, dress, and make the bed. She would probably go into town later, once she'd discovered the times for bus to Aldington. We it still be on the half hour as it used to be 5 years before? Before she left the bedroom, she stopped and picked up her bag. The hemp one with the flowers. Light but sturdy enough for the things she needed. Carefully walking down the narrow staircase, she held firmly on the bannister rail. She would not want to fall, as Mrs Threlfall had once done. As she said at the time, it wasn't the pain of the fall so much as the feeling of causing such a fuss. It would simply not do to have a fall on her very first morning in the cottage. In the hallway, a flyer for the fete at the local school. Nothing addressed personally to her a yet. Why would there be? She was reading the details of the tombola when she walked into the kitchen and saw the body. She thought of herself as quite a tough old girl. Her Freddy used to call her Boudicca. But she could not help but take a seat by the fridge, and she surveryed the scene. A young man. Blood coming from his head. Her immediate thoughts were not who is this man? Or how had he come to be lying dead on her as-yet unmopped floor. It was what should she do next?
